+# SECTION: session-duplicate
+
+Rules used by *Duplicate* in a tab's context menu, with the same
+syntax and expansion as *session-restore*. They are evaluated when
+the tab is duplicated (for at most two seconds), and the new tab
+re-runs the resulting programs without the saved screen content.
+Programs without a rule here fall back to their *session-restore*
+rule.
+
+Example:
+
+```
+[session-duplicate]
+claude=claude --resume "$(jq -r .sessionId "${CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR:-$HOME/.claude}/sessions/$FOOT_PID.json")" --fork-session
+```
+
+# SECTION: tabs
+
+Several terminals (tabs) can share one window. Every window has a
+sidebar listing its tabs. Session restore brings each window back
+with all its tabs, split views and sidebar state.
+
+The sidebar lists every tab on its own line; the panes of a split
+are joined by a bracket. Mouse actions: left-click a tab to select it
+(or focus that pane of a split); *Control*-click or *Shift*-click to
+select several (right-click a selected tab to split or close them
+together); scroll the wheel to switch to the previous/next tab (drag
+the scrollbar on the outer edge to move through a long list);
+right-click the empty part of the sidebar to sort the tabs by name,
+most recent use, program or opening order (pinned tabs stay first);
+middle-click to close; right-click for a menu (close, kill, duplicate, split, move
+out of split, unsplit all) driven by mouse or
+*Up*/*Down*/*Return*/*Escape*.
+
+Drag a tab to reorder it, or out of the sidebar onto any foot
+window: near a pane's left, right, top or bottom edge it is split in
+on that side (top and bottom stack the panes vertically); in the
+middle of a pane it is added as a tab; over a sidebar it is inserted
+where the line shows. Dropped anywhere else it opens in its own
+window. Dragging a pane of the current split moves it within the
+split, or out of it when dropped in the middle.
+
+Drag the sidebar's inner edge to resize it. The bottom row has *+*
+for a new tab and an arrow that hides the sidebar; a hidden sidebar
+leaves a sliver at the bottom corner that grows into an arrow on
+hover and shows the sidebar again.
+
+The compositor's close action (e.g. a window manager's close-window
+key) closes the current tab; closing the last tab closes the window.
+
+Hovering a tab shows an *×* to close it; double-click a tab to rename
+it. Pinned tabs stay at the top of the list and ask before closing.
+Right-click *+* to reopen the most recently closed tab (the last ten
+are kept, with their screen and relaunch rules as in session restore).
+
+In a split, drag the gap between panes to resize them, and use
+*Broadcast input* from a pane's menu to type into all of its panes at
+once; the gaps turn red while it is on.
+
+When a command that ran for 10 seconds or more finishes in a tab you
+aren't looking at, the tab gets a *✓*, and if its window isn't
+focused a desktop notification is sent; clicking it brings the tab
+to the front. This needs the shell to mark its prompts (OSC 133;A).
+
+In a split view, the first click on an unfocused pane only focuses
+it.
+
+Each tab's shell gets *FOOT_TAB_ID*; *footclient --focus-tab* brings
+that tab to the front, e.g. from a notification action.
+
+*Kill* sends SIGKILL to the tab's foreground process group and its
+shell, after a confirmation. *Close* hangs up as when closing a
+window. *Duplicate* opens a new tab running what the tab runs, see
+*session-duplicate*.
+
+*width*
+	Default sidebar width, in pixels (scaled). Default: _200_.
+
+*show*
+	Boolean. Whether new windows show the sidebar. Default: _no_.
+
+*position*
+	Sidebar side: _left_ or _right_. Default: _left_.
+
+*animations*
+	Boolean. Short sidebar animations (the current-tab highlight
+	glides, the sidebar slides in). They only redraw the sidebar.
+	Default: _yes_.
+
+*activity*
+	Boolean. Mark background tabs that print new lines. Redraws in
+	place, such as spinners and status lines, don't count. Default:
+	_yes_.
+
